WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.975
Practical Prophets. Season two. The Book of Judges. Part 1.

00:00:07.075 --> 00:00:10.675
Chapter 1. The Country After Joshua.

00:00:12.325 --> 00:00:22.075
Joshua was dead. There was land to inherit, a covenant to keep, and no single commander left to tell Israel how to finish the work.

00:00:22.375 --> 00:00:59.230
The tribes asked God who should fight the Canaanites first. Judah was chosen. Judah invited Simeon to help in both their allotted territories; together they won at Bezek, killing ten thousand Canaanites and Perizzites according to the account. The ruler Adoni-bezek fled, but was caught and had his thumbs and big toes cut off. He remembered seventy kings he had mutilated and forced to gather food under his table. As he had treated them, he said, God had now treated him. He was brought to Jerusalem and died there.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:26.220
Judah attacked Jerusalem, put its inhabitants to the sword and burned it. Campaigns followed in the hill country, the Negev and the lowland. At Hebron, formerly Kiriath-arba, Judah struck Sheshai, Ahiman and Talmai. At Debir, formerly Kiriath-sepher, Caleb promised his daughter Achsah in marriage to its conqueror. Othniel son of Kenaz took the city and married her.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:42.720
Achsah asked Caleb for springs because her land was in the dry southern country. He granted her the upper and lower springs. Even in a military catalogue, a question of water could determine whether an inheritance was livable.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:58.895
The Kenites descended from Moses' father-in-law moved from the City of Palms to the wilderness south of Arad. Judah and Simeon destroyed Zephath, naming it Hormah. Judah took Gaza, Ashkelon and Ekron with their territories.

00:01:59.195 --> 00:02:16.470
Then the report begins to unravel. Judah held the hill country but could not expel the people of the valley with their iron chariots. Caleb received Hebron and drove out the three sons of Anak. Benjamin did not remove the Jebusites of Jerusalem.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:33.545
Joseph's descendants attacked Bethel, then called Luz. They promised to spare a man and his household if he revealed the entrance. He did, and they honored their agreement. The man founded another town named Luz in Hittite territory.

00:02:33.845 --> 00:03:03.300
One tribe after another failed to drive out the inhabitants of its assigned towns. Manasseh left Canaanites at Beth-shean, Taanach, Dor, Ibleam and Megiddo; Ephraim at Gezer; Zebulun at Kitron and Nahalol; Asher at Acco, Sidon, Ahlab, Achzib, Helbah, Aphek and Rehob; Naphtali at Beth-shemesh and Beth-anath. Some conquered peoples became forced laborers, rather than being expelled.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:20.425
Dan was driven into the hills by Amorites who held the valley. The Amorite centers at Har-heres, Aijalon and Shaalbim remained until Joseph's house imposed tribute. The account gives the Amorite boundary from Akrabbim and Sela upward.

00:03:20.725 --> 00:03:28.025
Israel had received a map of inheritance. Judges begins with a map of what was still contested.

00:03:28.825 --> 00:03:49.140
Several incidents here also appear in Joshua. The opening should not be read as proof that every battle happened after Joshua's funeral. More important is the repeated distinction between taking a city and actually controlling its inhabitants. The phrase “did not drive out” becomes the chapter's refrain.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.765
Chapter 2. The Generation That Forgot.

00:03:55.415 --> 00:04:02.565
The messenger of God arrived at a place later named Bochim, “Weepers,” with an accusation.

00:04:02.865 --> 00:04:24.480
God had delivered Israel from Egypt and brought the people into the promised land. He had pledged not to break His covenant. But Israel had been told not to make covenants with the land's peoples and to tear down their altars. The Israelites had not obeyed. Those peoples would become a snare, and their gods a trap.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.730
The people wept when they heard the message and offered sacrifices there.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:58.670
Then the narrative turns backward, as though it wants to explain how the nation reached this moment. Joshua had dismissed the tribes to possess their respective inheritances. The Israelites served God throughout his lifetime and that of the elders who remembered the mighty acts of the Exodus and settlement. Joshua died at 110. He was buried in Timnath-heres, in Ephraim's hill country north of Mount Gaash.

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:02.070
And eventually the elders died too.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:09.420
A new generation arose, the book says, that did not know God or the deeds performed for Israel.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:28.145
The problem was no longer simply a missing commander. The people had inherited land and family names, but the memory of the covenant was no longer binding them together as it had bound their elders. They turned to Baal and Ashtaroth, the deities of neighboring peoples.

00:05:28.445 --> 00:05:43.545
The narrator describes foreign domination and plunder as the consequence of disloyalty. Israel could not stand before its enemies. Yet when its people groaned under oppression, God raised up judges to rescue them.

00:05:43.845 --> 00:05:51.320
The rescues worked while the judge lived. They did not necessarily change what happened after the judge died.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:06:06.745
The people returned to foreign worship and abandoned the commandments, sometimes becoming more corrupt than before. God left nations in the country, the narrative says, as a continuing test of Israel's loyalty.

00:06:07.045 --> 00:06:14.370
A recurring structure was now in place: wrongdoing, defeat, distress, rescue, and renewed decline.

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:23.620
The book will show leaders who save their people without solving the deeper problem—and sometimes add new problems of their own.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:47.685
Shoftim, “Judges,” includes military deliverers and regional leaders, not merely courtroom officials. This chapter provides the theological framework for the entire book. Its claim that God allowed remaining nations to test Israel is the narrator's interpretation of events, not an independently verified archaeological explanation.

00:06:48.785 --> 00:06:52.010
Chapter 3. The Left-Handed Man.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:07:15.700
The remaining peoples of Canaan meant that Israel's younger generation lived beside nations capable of war: Philistine rulers, Canaanites, Sidonians, Hivites, Hittites, Amorites, Perizzites and Jebusites. Marriages linked households across these communities, and religious loyalties shifted with them.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:39.265
The first rescuer was Othniel, Caleb's relative, whom readers of Joshua already knew as the conqueror of Debir. Israel had spent eight years under Cushan-rishathaim, king of Aram-naharaim. The people cried out; God's spirit came upon Othniel, who defeated the king. The land rested forty years. Othniel died.

00:07:39.565 --> 00:07:52.790
Then another crisis began. Eglon king of Moab allied with Ammon and Amalek, defeated Israel and occupied the City of Palms. For eighteen years the Israelites served him.

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:58.940
God raised up Ehud son of Gera, a Benjaminite who used his left hand.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:15.340
Israel sent Ehud with tribute for Eglon. Ehud made a short, double-edged sword and hid it on his right thigh beneath his clothing. He delivered the payment and dismissed the other carriers, then turned back from the carved stones near Gilgal.

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:21.215
He told Eglon he had a secret message. The king dismissed everyone else.

00:08:21.515 --> 00:08:40.565
In his cool upper chamber Eglon sat alone. Ehud announced a message from God. As the king stood, Ehud drew his hidden sword with his left hand and drove it into Eglon's belly. The blade disappeared into the king's body. Ehud left it there, shut the doors and locked them.

00:08:40.865 --> 00:08:56.315
The servants assumed their king was attending to a private bodily need. They waited, embarrassed to interrupt. Only when the delay became impossible to ignore did they unlock the chamber and find Eglon dead on the floor.

00:08:56.615 --> 00:09:18.255
By then Ehud had escaped past the carved stones to Seirah. He blew a horn in Ephraim's hill country, gathered men behind him and seized the Jordan crossings. The Moabites were cut off; the narrative numbers the slain at about ten thousand. Moab was subdued, and the land rested eighty years.

00:09:18.555 --> 00:09:30.280
The next deliverer received just one verse. Shamgar son of Anath struck six hundred Philistines with an ox goad, a farmer's implement, and also saved Israel.

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:44.330
Three leaders, three kinds of victory. Othniel had been an established warrior. Ehud turned the details of a diplomatic mission into a deadly surprise. Shamgar fought with an ordinary tool.

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:49.180
The book refuses to reduce rescue to one kind of hero.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:10:10.520
Ehud's left-handedness matters to the plot: the sword was on his right thigh. The locked room and the servants' mistaken assumption explain how he escaped. Those details are explicit in the source. Shamgar's entire account is one verse, but its ox goad and six hundred Philistines are not optional.

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:15.270
Chapter 4. The General Who Fled on Foot.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:33.020
After Ehud died, Jabin king of Canaan at Hazor gained power over Israel. His commander Sisera had nine hundred iron chariots and based himself at Harosheth-goiim. For twenty years Israel suffered under that force.

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:47.445
At the time a prophetess named Deborah was judging Israel. She was the wife of Lappidoth and sat beneath a palm tree between Ramah and Bethel in Ephraim's hill country. People came to her for judgment.

00:10:47.745 --> 00:11:04.745
Deborah summoned Barak son of Abinoam from Kedesh in Naphtali and gave him a command from God: gather ten thousand from Naphtali and Zebulun at Mount Tabor. Sisera would come with his chariots to the Kishon River, where God would deliver him.

00:11:05.045 --> 00:11:08.745
Barak would go only if Deborah accompanied him.

00:11:09.045 --> 00:11:18.170
She agreed, but warned that the honor of the campaign would not finally belong to him: Sisera would fall into the hand of a woman.

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:36.820
As Barak gathered the ten thousand, the story briefly introduced Heber the Kenite, who had separated from his kinsmen and pitched his tent near Kedesh. His family had peaceful relations with Jabin's court. It is a small piece of information that will soon prove decisive.

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:44.595
Sisera heard that Barak had gone to Tabor. He assembled his nine hundred chariots and troops at the Kishon.

00:11:44.895 --> 00:11:48.220
“This is the day,” Deborah told Barak.

00:11:48.520 --> 00:12:01.695
The Israelites descended the mountain and attacked. The narrative credits God with throwing Sisera's army into confusion. The soldiers were destroyed as Barak pursued them toward Harosheth-goiim.

00:12:01.995 --> 00:12:05.745
Sisera abandoned his chariot and fled on foot.

00:12:06.045 --> 00:12:23.920
He reached the tent of Jael, wife of Heber. Believing the household friendly, he accepted her invitation to enter. She covered him with a rug. He asked for water; she gave him milk. Then he asked her to stand at the entrance and deny that anyone was inside.

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:31.595
When he fell asleep, Jael took a tent peg and a hammer and drove the peg through his temple into the ground.

00:12:31.895 --> 00:12:41.920
Barak arrived in pursuit. Jael came out to meet him and invited him to see the man he sought. Sisera lay dead inside the tent.

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:47.995
Israel's strength against Jabin grew until the king was defeated and destroyed.

00:12:48.295 --> 00:12:58.370
The battle was led by Barak. The command came through Deborah. But Sisera's death came at the hands of Jael, precisely as Deborah had warned.

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:17.120
Deborah is established as prophetess and judge before Barak appears. Jael's family's good relations with Jabin explain why Sisera sought refuge there. The next chapter sings about this same victory in a different literary form, rather than describing a second battle.

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:21.295
Chapter 5. The Victory Song.

00:13:22.945 --> 00:13:28.745
When Deborah and Barak sang of the battle, they did not begin with Sisera's defeat.

00:13:29.045 --> 00:13:32.570
They began by blessing those who volunteered.

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:57.685
The song reaches back to God's appearance from Seir and Edom, with trembling mountains and rain from heaven, recalling Sinai. It remembers the time of Shamgar and Jael as one when roads were deserted, travelers used byways and ordinary life had become dangerous. The poem speaks of a lack of weapons among forty thousand Israelites.

00:13:57.985 --> 00:14:01.385
Deborah arose as a “mother in Israel.”

00:14:01.685 --> 00:14:18.210
Then the song becomes an account of who came when the call went out. Ephraim and Benjamin sent men. Machir contributed leaders; Zebulun brought commanders; Issachar joined Barak. Zebulun and Naphtali risked their lives at the battle heights.

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:21.435
But the song remembers absence too.

00:14:21.735 --> 00:14:34.585
Reuben remained among the sheepfolds, listening to the flocks while wrestling with its choices. Gilead stayed across the Jordan. Dan stayed by the ships. Asher remained at the coast.

00:14:34.885 --> 00:14:47.860
The kings of Canaan fought near Taanach and the waters of Megiddo. The stars in their courses are pictured fighting Sisera. The Kishon swept away the enemy. The horses thundered in flight.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:54.085
A messenger of God cursed Meroz for failing to come to the aid of the fighters.

00:14:54.385 --> 00:15:05.060
Jael received the opposite judgment. The song blessed her among women, revisiting the milk, the peg and the hammer and Sisera's collapse at her feet.

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:08.885
Then it moved unexpectedly to a window.

00:15:09.185 --> 00:15:23.585
Sisera's mother waited behind the lattice, wondering why her son's chariot had not returned. Her attendants supposed the warriors must still be dividing embroidered garments and women taken as captive spoils.

00:15:23.885 --> 00:15:29.010
The listener knew what they did not. The chariot was never coming back.

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:39.460
The poem ended with a wish that God's enemies perish and those who love Him rise like the sun in strength. The land rested forty years.

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:54.160
The prose account told who defeated Sisera. The song tells how people chose to remember the battle: praising those who acted, blaming those who stood aside, and showing a mother who still expected victory.

00:15:54.960 --> 00:16:17.750
This chapter is biblical poetry, not a second literal campaign. The named tribes that fought or declined to fight, the curse of Meroz and the scene of Sisera's mother are important parts of the account. The mother's attendants explicitly anticipate captive women as spoils of war. That harsh expectation should not be softened away.

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:22.025
Chapter 6. The Man in the Winepress.

00:16:23.675 --> 00:16:28.225
Midian's raids reduced Israelite farming to survival.

00:16:28.525 --> 00:16:45.700
For seven years, when the people planted crops, Midianites, Amalekites and eastern groups arrived with animals, tents and camels. They devastated fields and left little food. Israelites hid in caves, mountain shelters and strongholds.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:57.525
The people cried out to God. First came a prophet, reminding them of the Exodus and charging them with failing to obey the command not to fear the gods of the Amorites.

00:16:57.825 --> 00:17:05.000
Then a divine messenger came to Ophrah and sat beneath a tree belonging to Joash the Abiezrite.

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:11.375
Joash's son Gideon was threshing wheat inside a winepress to hide it from Midian.

00:17:11.675 --> 00:17:16.000
“The Lord is with you, mighty warrior,” the messenger said.

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:27.000
Gideon did not accept the greeting easily. If God was with Israel, why were the people suffering? Where were the wonders their ancestors had described?

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:38.875
He was told to save Israel from Midian. Gideon protested that his clan was weak within Manasseh and he was the least in his family. God promised to be with him.

00:17:39.175 --> 00:17:59.865
He asked for a sign, prepared a young goat and unleavened bread with broth, and presented them to the visitor. The angel told him to set the food on a rock and pour over the liquid. Fire rose from the rock when the visitor touched it with his staff, consuming the offering. The messenger disappeared.

00:18:00.165 --> 00:18:11.265
Gideon feared he would die after seeing God's messenger. God told him to be at peace. He built an altar and named it Adonai-shalom, “the Lord is peace.”

00:18:11.565 --> 00:18:25.040
That night he received another command: tear down his father's altar to Baal, cut down the Asherah beside it, build an altar to Israel's God and offer the second bull using the cut wood.

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:32.815
Gideon obeyed with ten servants—but in darkness, out of fear of his household and the townspeople.

00:18:33.115 --> 00:18:47.490
In the morning the men demanded his death. Joash challenged them: if Baal was truly a god, why did he need human beings to defend his altar? Gideon was given the name Jerubbaal, “Let Baal contend.”

00:18:47.790 --> 00:19:01.690
Midianites and their allies then assembled in the Valley of Jezreel. The spirit of God clothed Gideon. He sounded the horn and summoned Abiezer, Manasseh, Asher, Zebulun and Naphtali.

00:19:01.990 --> 00:19:04.390
Yet he still sought proof.

00:19:04.690 --> 00:19:21.240
He put a fleece on the threshing floor and asked God to make it wet with dew while the surrounding ground stayed dry. It happened; he wrung a bowlful of water from it. Then he asked for the reverse, dry fleece with wet ground. That happened too.

00:19:21.540 --> 00:19:28.440
The man called a mighty warrior was afraid before the battle, and he said so through his requests.

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:32.840
The story would not become stronger by hiding that fear.

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:55.505
Gideon begins in a winepress hiding wheat. His first demolition is against the altar in his own household, carried out at night because he is afraid. The fleece tests follow his call to arms. The Hebrew depicts God's spirit as “clothing” Gideon—a vivid image of empowerment rather than a description of protective armor.

00:19:56.605 --> 00:19:59.355
Chapter 7. Three Hundred.

00:20:01.005 --> 00:20:10.805
Gideon had assembled an army by En-harod. The Midianites lay in the valley below, a vast collection of men, tents and camels.

00:20:11.105 --> 00:20:15.005
God told Gideon that he had too many soldiers.

00:20:15.305 --> 00:20:24.630
If Israel won with such a force, the people might credit themselves instead of God. Gideon was ordered to allow the fearful to leave.

00:20:24.930 --> 00:20:29.180
Twenty-two thousand went home. Ten thousand remained.

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:31.680
That was still too many.

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:50.570
Gideon brought the men to water and separated them by the manner in which they drank. Three hundred lifted water to their mouths and lapped it; the others knelt down. The three hundred were selected. Everyone else departed, leaving the small force with provisions and horns.

00:20:50.870 --> 00:20:59.870
That night God ordered Gideon to attack. If he still feared, he could go with his servant Purah to the edge of the camp and listen.

00:21:00.170 --> 00:21:01.695
He went.

00:21:01.995 --> 00:21:14.995
One Midianite told another a dream. A loaf of barley bread had rolled into the camp and overturned a tent. The other man interpreted it as a sign that Gideon would defeat Midian.

00:21:15.295 --> 00:21:20.070
Gideon worshipped and returned to order the Israelites forward.

00:21:20.370 --> 00:21:33.295
He divided the three hundred into three companies. Each man took a horn, an empty jar and a torch hidden inside the jar. Gideon told them to watch him and repeat his actions.

00:21:33.595 --> 00:21:39.345
They reached the enemy at the beginning of the middle watch, just as the sentries changed.

00:21:39.645 --> 00:21:48.495
Horns sounded. Jars shattered. Torches appeared suddenly around the camp. The Israelites shouted for God and Gideon.

00:21:48.795 --> 00:22:03.570
They stood in place while the Midianite forces panicked. According to the narrative, God turned the enemy soldiers' swords against one another, and the camp fled toward Beth-shittah, Zererah, Abel-meholah and Tabbath.

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:26.335
Men from Naphtali, Asher and Manasseh joined the pursuit. Gideon called Ephraim to seize the water crossings and the Jordan. Ephraim captured two Midianite princes, Oreb and Zeeb, killing them at places later known as the Rock of Oreb and the Winepress of Zeeb. Their heads were brought to Gideon beyond the river.

00:22:26.635 --> 00:22:35.885
Three hundred men had thrown a far larger army into confusion. But the pursuit was not over. Two kings remained alive.

00:22:36.185 --> 00:22:43.835
And a man raised up to save Israel was about to face a different test: what victory would do to him.

00:22:44.635 --> 00:23:07.625
The biblical reason for reducing Gideon's force is explicit: Israel must not claim that its own strength achieved salvation. The water test distinguishes three hundred men, but the text does not say they were the best soldiers or most alert. The dream of the barley loaf and Gideon's servant Purah belong to the preparation for battle.

00:23:08.725 --> 00:23:12.175
Chapter 8. What Victory Did to Gideon.

00:23:13.825 --> 00:23:20.375
Gideon's victory against Midian had not united Israel as completely as it seemed.

00:23:20.675 --> 00:23:36.050
The men of Ephraim accused him of failing to call them to the first battle. Gideon answered with praise: Ephraim had captured Oreb and Zeeb; was not its gleaning better than Abiezer's whole harvest? Their anger subsided.

00:23:36.350 --> 00:24:02.090
But Gideon was still pursuing two Midianite kings, Zebah and Zalmunna. He crossed the Jordan with his three hundred, exhausted. At Succoth he asked for bread. The leaders refused to assist a force that had not yet captured its enemies. Gideon promised to punish them with desert thorns. At Penuel he met the same refusal and threatened to tear down their tower.

00:24:02.390 --> 00:24:19.465
The kings were at Karkor with about fifteen thousand men. The narrative says a hundred and twenty thousand of the eastern force had already fallen. Gideon approached by the road east of Nobah and Jogbehah, surprised the camp and captured both kings.

00:24:19.765 --> 00:24:36.490
Returning, he found a young man from Succoth and obtained the names of seventy-seven elders. Gideon confronted them with his captives, then punished the city's elders with thorns and briers. He demolished Penuel's tower and killed men there.

00:24:36.790 --> 00:25:05.205
He asked the captured kings about men they had killed at Tabor. They said the victims had resembled him, like sons of a king. They were Gideon's brothers. If the kings had spared them, Gideon replied, he would have spared the kings. He told his firstborn Jether to execute them, but the youth was afraid. The kings challenged Gideon to do it himself. Gideon killed them and took the crescent ornaments from their camels.

00:25:05.505 --> 00:25:13.530
Israel offered Gideon and his descendants the throne. Gideon refused: God, he said, would rule over them.

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:33.095
Then he asked the men for golden earrings from the spoils. They gave seventeen hundred shekels' weight of gold, besides other ornaments and royal clothing. Gideon made an ephod and placed it at Ophrah. The people went astray after it. It became a trap to the man who had saved them.

00:25:33.395 --> 00:25:50.120
The country rested forty years. Gideon had seventy sons by many wives and another son, Abimelech, by a concubine at Shechem. When Gideon died and was buried in Ophrah, Israel turned to Baal-berith and neglected his family.

00:25:50.420 --> 00:26:00.870
Gideon had refused to be king. But he had left behind the wealth, household and religious object of a man whose power was not easily forgotten.

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:05.070
And one of his sons was determined to become king.

00:26:05.870 --> 00:26:24.935
Gideon's refusal of kingship is genuine speech recorded by the text. So are his punitive actions at Succoth and Penuel and the ephod that becomes a religious snare. A heroic biography that suppresses those acts would leave the reader unable to discuss the Gideon of Judges.

00:26:26.035 --> 00:26:29.060
Chapter 9. The King of Thorns.

00:26:30.710 --> 00:26:44.135
Abimelech went to Shechem, where his mother's relatives lived, and asked its leaders a question. Would they rather be ruled by Gideon's seventy sons or by one man who was their own flesh and blood?

00:26:44.435 --> 00:26:46.860
They chose their relative.

00:26:47.160 --> 00:27:01.760
The sanctuary of Baal-berith supplied seventy pieces of silver. With that money Abimelech hired men, went to Ophrah and murdered his brothers upon one stone. The youngest, Jotham, escaped by hiding.

00:27:02.060 --> 00:27:08.235
The people of Shechem and Beth-millo made Abimelech king beneath the great tree at Shechem.

00:27:08.535 --> 00:27:29.875
Jotham climbed Mount Gerizim and addressed them through a fable. The trees sought a king. The olive tree refused to abandon its oil, the fig tree its fruit, and the vine its wine. Only the bramble accepted, promising its shadow as shelter and threatening to burn Lebanon's cedars if rejected.

00:27:30.175 --> 00:27:45.125
Jotham condemned Shechem for betraying the house of Gideon, who had risked his life for Israel. Unless the choice was just, he declared, fire would consume Abimelech and the men of Shechem through one another. He fled to Beer.

00:27:45.425 --> 00:28:06.965
Abimelech ruled three years. The alliance deteriorated into treachery. Men of Shechem ambushed travelers and robbed them. Gaal son of Ebed arrived with his brothers and denounced Abimelech at a festival after the grape harvest. Zebul, Abimelech's city official, secretly sent a warning to his ruler.

00:28:07.265 --> 00:28:22.115
Abimelech advanced by night in four companies. At dawn Gaal saw men coming down from the mountains. Zebul mocked him, suggesting they were shadows, then challenged the man who had publicly promised to fight Abimelech.

00:28:22.415 --> 00:28:35.640
Gaal led the men out, was defeated, and was expelled. The next day Abimelech trapped those leaving the city, captured Shechem, killed its inhabitants, destroyed the city and scattered salt over it.

00:28:35.940 --> 00:28:52.715
About a thousand men and women sought shelter in a fortified sanctuary at the tower of Shechem. Abimelech ordered his men to follow him in gathering branches from Mount Zalmon. They heaped wood against the stronghold and burned it with the people inside.

00:28:53.015 --> 00:29:05.565
He then attacked Thebez. Its inhabitants sheltered in a strong tower. As Abimelech approached to set it on fire, a woman dropped an upper millstone on his head, breaking his skull.

00:29:05.865 --> 00:29:15.190
He ordered his armor-bearer to kill him so no one would say a woman had slain him. The young man did so. The soldiers went home.

00:29:15.490 --> 00:29:24.465
The narrator says God returned Abimelech's wickedness and Shechem's betrayal upon their own heads, fulfilling Jotham's curse.

00:29:24.765 --> 00:29:36.840
The only ruler to claim a crown in this part of Judges has left cities burned, brothers murdered, and a final command designed to conceal the woman who mortally wounded him.

00:29:37.640 --> 00:30:00.705
Jotham's parable is a political argument about the kind of person who demands power. The useful trees refuse; the bramble offers shelter it cannot reasonably provide. The story then narrates how Abimelech and Shechem destroy one another. The woman's millstone and Abimelech's concern for his reputation are explicit parts of his end.

00:30:01.805 --> 00:30:05.430
Chapter 10. The Commander No One Wanted.

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:10.480
For a time the record becomes quieter.

00:30:10.780 --> 00:30:21.230
Tola son of Puah, son of Dodo, from Issachar, judged Israel twenty-three years. He lived at Shamir in Ephraim's hills and was buried there.

00:30:21.530 --> 00:30:33.130
Jair the Gileadite followed for twenty-two years. His thirty sons rode thirty donkeys and had thirty towns known as Havvoth-jair. He died and was buried at Kamon.

00:30:33.430 --> 00:30:51.455
Then Israel's religious allegiances fractured again. The people served Baal, Ashtaroth and deities associated with Aram, Sidon, Moab, Ammon and the Philistines. The account describes the consequence as oppression by Philistines and Ammonites.

00:30:51.755 --> 00:31:02.580
Across the Jordan, the Israelites of Gilead suffered for eighteen years. Ammonite forces also crossed west to fight Judah, Benjamin and Ephraim.

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:23.695
The Israelites confessed their disloyalty and cried out to God. The answer recalled earlier rescues from Egyptians, Amorites, Ammonites, Philistines, Sidonians, Amalekites and Maonites. They had repeatedly turned away afterward. Why not ask the gods they had chosen to save them?

00:31:23.995 --> 00:31:38.420
The people acknowledged their wrongdoing and asked for rescue despite the punishment they deserved. They removed the foreign gods and served Israel's God. The narrator says their misery became unbearable to Him.

00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:43.995
Ammon gathered its armies in Gilead. Israel assembled at Mizpah.

00:31:44.295 --> 00:31:54.345
The leaders of Gilead asked one another who would begin the fight. They promised that the man who did so would become head over all Gilead.

00:31:54.645 --> 00:31:56.920
They needed a commander.

00:31:57.220 --> 00:32:02.295
The man most suited to the task was someone they had once driven away.

00:32:03.095 --> 00:32:21.435
The brief notices about Tola and Jair belong to the source, including their durations and burials. The long list of foreign gods shows how broadly Israel's allegiance had shifted. The chapter ends with an offer of leadership but withholds the name of the man who will accept it.

00:32:22.535 --> 00:32:25.060
Chapter 11. The Vow.

00:32:26.710 --> 00:32:42.185
Jephthah was a powerful warrior from Gilead and the son of a woman described as a prostitute. His father's other sons drove him out of the family inheritance. He went to Tob, where men without settled standing joined him.

00:32:42.485 --> 00:32:48.535
When Ammon threatened Gilead, the elders went to retrieve the man they had rejected.

00:32:48.835 --> 00:33:06.860
Jephthah asked why they came now, in trouble, when they had driven him from his father's house. They offered to make him leader over Gilead. He required a binding promise that if God granted victory, he would remain their head. They swore, and he was installed at Mizpah.

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:21.485
Before fighting, Jephthah sent messages to the Ammonite king. The king claimed the territory between the Arnon, the Jabbok and the Jordan had been taken from Ammon at the Exodus and demanded its return.

00:33:21.785 --> 00:33:59.565
Jephthah answered with a carefully detailed history. Israel had sought passage through Edom and Moab but had been refused. It had traveled around those lands and approached Sihon king of the Amorites at Heshbon. Sihon attacked, and Israel defeated him. The country gained had been Amorite, not Ammonite. Jephthah invoked Chemosh in a rhetorical challenge about whose deity granted territory. Balak king of Moab had never made the claim, he added, and Israel had held the towns of Heshbon, Aroer and Arnon for three hundred years.

00:33:59.865 --> 00:34:02.990
The Ammonite king refused to listen.

00:34:03.290 --> 00:34:20.515
The spirit of God came upon Jephthah as he marched through Gilead and Manasseh toward the enemy. Then he made a vow: if he returned victorious, whatever came first from his house to greet him would be devoted to God and offered as a burnt offering.

00:34:20.815 --> 00:34:27.440
Jephthah defeated Ammon across twenty cities, from Aroer toward Minnith and Abel-cheramim.

00:34:27.740 --> 00:34:29.740
He returned home.

00:34:30.040 --> 00:34:36.540
His daughter came out to greet him with tambourines and dancing. She was his only child.

00:34:36.840 --> 00:34:48.240
Jephthah tore his clothes. He had spoken a vow he could not withdraw. She accepted his words but asked two months in the mountains with her companions to mourn her virginity.

00:34:48.540 --> 00:35:01.590
After two months she returned. The text says her father fulfilled the vow, adding that she had never known a man. Israelite daughters subsequently commemorated her four days each year.

00:35:01.890 --> 00:35:12.340
The girl is never named. The father is remembered as a deliverer; the daughter is remembered because his vow consumed the possibility of her future.

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:37.455
Jephthah's daughter's fate is debated in Jewish commentary. Many understand the text as describing her death through sacrifice; others understand lifelong dedication and separation from marriage. The book does not narrate a rescue. A faithful version should present the recorded words and the unresolved interpretive question without inventing an outcome.
